text:
Why this chapter matters
Gradient descent specifies how parameters should change once their gradients are known. Backpropagation computes those gradients efficiently by applying the chain rule through the operations that produced the loss. Without this procedure, training a neural network one parameter at a time would be prohibitively expensive.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

distinguish a forward pass from a backward pass;
interpret a computation graph;
apply the chain rule to a small network;
explain why backpropagation reuses intermediate values;
compare analytical, finite-difference, and automatic derivatives;
inspect gradients in PyTorch;
explain activation memory and gradient checkpointing.



Running example
We will use a three-operation computation:
\[
a=3x,\qquad b=a+4,\qquad L=b^2.
\]
It is small enough to calculate by hand, but it contains the same structure as a larger network: operations are composed in a forward direction, and the derivative of the final loss is propagated backwards through them.

Backpropagation. A method for calculating derivatives of a model’s scalar loss with respect to its parameters by applying the chain rule backwards through the recorded forward computation. It calculates gradients; an optimiser uses those gradients to choose parameter updates.

Level 4 - Research & Systems

Weight decay depends on the optimiser and parameter group
Decoupled weight decay separates shrinkage from the gradient-based Adam update (Loshchilov and Hutter 2019). Implementations commonly exclude biases and normalisation scale or offset parameters from decay. That is an architectural choice, not a universal rule. A reproducible configuration records parameter groups and their penalties rather than reporting one global number.
At scale, weight decay adds little arithmetic but interacts with fused optimiser kernels, sharded optimiser state, and checkpoint formats. Changing the optimiser implementation can change numerical order and defaults even when the headline hyperparameters match.


Dropout changes randomness and execution
Dropout was introduced as stochastic training of many thinned networks with a scaled full network at evaluation (Srivastava et al. 2014). Its effect depends on placement and rate. Attention dropout, residual dropout, and feed-forward dropout mask different tensors. Modern architectures with large datasets, normalisation, augmentation, or other stochastic components may use little or no dropout; the original empirical result is not a mandate for every network.
Distributed workers need independent but reproducible random streams. Accidentally sharing masks can reduce intended variation, while restoring a checkpoint without random-number state changes the continuation. Evaluation must disable dropout unless stochastic inference is deliberately used and reported.


Augmentation quality is a substantive property
Mixup and image transformations are often implemented cheaply on accelerators, but other augmentations can become data-pipeline bottlenecks. More importantly, throughput does not establish label preservation. Cropping may remove the object that defines an image label; temporal warping may change an event; language substitutions may alter meaning. Augmentation policies need domain evidence and error analysis, not only validation gains.
Synthetic data from another model add further dependencies: generator coverage, inherited bias, privacy, provenance, and duplication. Calling generated examples augmentation does not remove these evaluation requirements.


Early stopping consumes validation information
Early stopping saves computation when later epochs do not improve the selected metric, but asynchronous evaluation and distributed checkpoints complicate the definition of the best step. Validation results may arrive after training has advanced. Systems must retain candidate checkpoints until selection is resolved and record whether patience is measured in epochs, optimiser steps, tokens, or evaluation events.
The chosen checkpoint can vary across seeds. Reporting only the best run combines regularisation selection with run selection. Comparisons should use matched budgets and a declared aggregation across runs.


A regularisation audit links mechanism to evidence
An audit should record:

the observed overfitting pattern and metric used to diagnose it;
each regularisation mechanism, location, strength, and schedule;
feature scaling and parameters excluded from penalties;
augmentation transformations and the evidence that labels remain valid;
training/evaluation mode and random-state handling for dropout;
validation frequency, patience, minimum improvement, and restored checkpoint;
the number of settings and runs compared;
task performance, calibration, subgroup errors, compute, memory, and latency;
final assessment on data untouched by regularisation selection.

Regularisation is supported when this comparison improves the intended transfer criterion under a fixed evaluation design. A smaller training–validation gap caused by making both errors worse is not an improvement.

Why this chapter matters
Training begins before the first parameter update. Initial weights determine the scale and diversity of the signals produced by the untrained network. If those signals shrink across layers, later layers receive little information. If they grow, activations may saturate or overflow. Backpropagation traverses the same chain in reverse, so the initial scale also affects whether early layers receive usable gradients.
Initialisation does not determine the final model by itself. It sets the starting conditions for an interaction among architecture, activation, normalisation, residual connections, optimiser, precision, and data. This chapter isolates that starting point and shows how to diagnose it.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

explain why identical initial values prevent hidden units from learning different features;
trace activation scale across a deep network before training;
explain vanishing and exploding gradients as products of local transformations;
derive Xavier and He variance rules under their stated assumptions;
distinguish fan-in, fan-out, and activation-specific gain;
implement and inspect initialisation in NumPy and PyTorch;
measure activation, saturation, and gradient statistics by layer;
explain why residual paths, normalisation, and numerical precision change the initialisation problem;
record sufficient evidence to diagnose an unstable start.



Running experiment
The chapter repeatedly propagates a batch of independent, standard-normal inputs through 30 width-128 layers. The network is deliberately deeper than needed for a practical toy prediction task because changes in scale become visible. Biases start at zero and ReLU is applied after each matrix multiplication.




Code
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.style.use("seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid")
blue, teal, orange, purple, grey = "#24527a", "#3a9188", "#d97745", "#7a5195", "#5f6b76"
rng = np.random.default_rng(71)
width, depth, batch = 128, 30, 256
inputs = rng.normal(size=(batch, width))

def forward_statistics(scale):
    h=inputs.copy(); standard_deviations=[]; active_fractions=[]; weights=[]; masks=[]
    for _ in range(depth):
        W=rng.normal(0,scale,size=(width,width)); z=h@W.T; h=np.maximum(0,z)
        standard_deviations.append(h.std()); active_fractions.append((z&gt;0).mean())
        weights.append(W); masks.append(z&gt;0)
    return np.array(standard_deviations),np.array(active_fractions),weights,masks

scales={"small: 0.02":0.02,"Xavier":np.sqrt(1/width),"He":np.sqrt(2/width),"large: 0.20":0.20}
scale_results={name:forward_statistics(scale) for name,scale in scales.items()}
fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(9,5))
for (name,result),colour in zip(scale_results.items(),[blue,grey,teal,orange]):
    ax.plot(range(1,depth+1),result[0],label=name,color=colour,linewidth=2.4)
ax.set_yscale("log"); ax.set_xlabel("Layer"); ax.set_ylabel("Activation standard deviation")
ax.set_title("Forward signal scale before training"); ax.legend()
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()



Figure 12.1



The small initialisation progressively removes variation. The large initialisation amplifies it. Xavier preserves variance for linear or approximately symmetric activations but loses scale under repeated ReLU gating. He initialisation compensates for that gating in this idealised setting and keeps the signal closer to its starting scale.

Why this chapter matters
Chapter 3 established the basic update \(\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t-\eta g_t\). That equation leaves several practical decisions unresolved. A mini-batch gradient is noisy. Different parameters can receive gradients on very different scales. One learning rate rarely works equally well at the beginning and end of training. Regularisation may be embedded in the gradient or applied as a separate parameter shrinkage. An optimiser specifies how these decisions are made over a sequence of steps.
Optimisers do not replace gradients. They transform current and past gradients into an update. Momentum accumulates a direction over time. Adam combines a moving average of gradients with a moving average of squared gradients. AdamW separates weight decay from that adaptive transformation. A learning-rate schedule changes the overall update scale as training progresses. The resulting path depends on the complete state of the optimiser, not only on the current parameter and gradient.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

distinguish a gradient from the update produced by an optimiser;
explain how momentum filters noisy gradients and accelerates motion in persistent directions;
calculate SGD with momentum and Adam updates, including Adam’s bias correction;
explain how adaptive second-moment estimates create coordinate-specific effective step sizes;
distinguish L2 regularisation from decoupled weight decay under adaptive optimisation;
interpret warm-up, constant, step, exponential, and cosine learning-rate schedules;
connect schedule units to batches, epochs, gradient accumulation, and distributed training;
identify the optimiser state required for an exact training restart;
design an audit that separates optimisation failure from implementation failure.



Running loss surface
The chapter repeatedly uses an elongated quadratic loss,
\[
L(x,y)=\frac{1}{2}(x^2+20y^2).
\]
The surface is twenty times steeper in the \(y\) direction than in the \(x\) direction. One global learning rate must therefore make progress along a shallow direction without becoming unstable along a steep one. This simple geometry exposes why update history and coordinate scaling can matter.




Code
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.style.use("seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid")
blue, teal, orange, purple, grey = "#24527a", "#3a9188", "#d97745", "#7a5195", "#5f6b76"

def quadratic_loss(theta):
    return 0.5 * (theta[0]**2 + 20 * theta[1]**2)

def quadratic_gradient(theta):
    return np.array([theta[0], 20 * theta[1]])

def optimise(method, steps=100):
    theta = np.array([-4.5, 1.6], dtype=float)
    velocity = np.zeros(2); first = np.zeros(2); second = np.zeros(2)
    path = [theta.copy()]
    for step in range(1, steps + 1):
        gradient = quadratic_gradient(theta)
        if method == "SGD":
            theta -= 0.08 * gradient
        elif method == "Momentum":
            velocity = 0.85 * velocity + gradient
            theta -= 0.03 * velocity
        else:
            first = 0.9 * first + 0.1 * gradient
            second = 0.999 * second + 0.001 * gradient**2
            first_hat = first / (1 - 0.9**step)
            second_hat = second / (1 - 0.999**step)
            theta -= 0.18 * first_hat / (np.sqrt(second_hat) + 1e-8)
        path.append(theta.copy())
    return np.array(path)

paths = {name: optimise(name) for name in ["SGD", "Momentum", "Adam"]}
x = np.linspace(-5.2, 1.5, 300); y = np.linspace(-2, 2, 300)
X, Y = np.meshgrid(x, y); Z = 0.5 * (X**2 + 20 * Y**2)
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(12, 4), sharex=True, sharey=True)
for ax, (name, path), colour in zip(axes, paths.items(), [orange, teal, purple]):
    ax.contour(X, Y, Z, levels=np.geomspace(0.04, 40, 14), cmap="Blues", linewidths=.75)
    ax.plot(path[:,0], path[:,1], color=colour, marker="o", markevery=10,
            markersize=3, linewidth=1.9)
    ax.scatter([0], [0], color=blue, s=45, zorder=3)
    ax.set_title(name); ax.set_xlabel("x")
axes[0].set_ylabel("y")
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()



Figure 14.1



The paths should not be read as a ranking. Their hyperparameters were selected to make each mechanism visible, and another loss surface can favour another configuration. The figure shows that an optimiser changes the trajectory even when every method receives the same exact gradient function.

Why this chapter matters
Neural networks operate on numerical arrays. The numbers alone do not identify what an axis means, which values belong to one observation, or whether two coordinates can be compared. Those meanings come from the representation and the operations that produce it.
Earlier chapters used vectors, matrices, and higher-dimensional tensors to explain layers, batches, normalisation, and training systems. This chapter consolidates those objects before the book turns to embeddings and sequences. It distinguishes mathematical dimension from tensor rank, follows the contracts attached to each axis, and shows how a model transforms one representation into another.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

distinguish scalars, vectors, matrices, and higher-order tensors;
interpret a tensor shape as a set of named axes rather than a tuple of sizes;
index, slice, reshape, transpose, broadcast, and reduce arrays without losing their intended meaning;
write the shape and index equations for a batched sequence transformation;
explain why reshaping and permuting can preserve values while changing their interpretation;
distinguish a tensor from the representation carried by that tensor;
explain how a change of coordinates can preserve information but alter individual values;
distinguish invariance from equivariance;
identify information loss, padding errors, layout costs, and misleading representation comparisons;
specify a representation audit for a model or data pipeline.



Running tensor
The chapter uses a constructed batch containing two sequences, four positions per sequence, and three measured features per position. Its shape is therefore \(2\times4\times3\), written more generally as \(B\times S\times D\) for batch, sequence position, and feature dimension.




Code
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from matplotlib.patches import Rectangle, FancyArrowPatch

plt.style.use("seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid")
blue, teal, orange, purple, grey = "#24527a", "#3a9188", "#d97745", "#7a5195", "#5f6b76"

fig,axes=plt.subplots(1,4,figsize=(12,3.3))
titles=["Scalar\nrank 0","Vector\nrank 1","Matrix\nrank 2","Tensor\nrank 3"]
for ax,title in zip(axes,titles):
    ax.set_title(title); ax.axis("off"); ax.set_aspect("equal")
axes[0].add_patch(Rectangle((.4,.4),.6,.6,facecolor=orange,edgecolor="white")); axes[0].text(.7,.7,"7",ha="center",va="center")
for j in range(4):
    axes[1].add_patch(Rectangle((.1+j*.34,.55),.3,.3,facecolor=teal,edgecolor="white"))
for i in range(3):
    for j in range(4): axes[2].add_patch(Rectangle((.1+j*.3,.2+(2-i)*.3),.28,.28,facecolor=blue,alpha=.55+.12*i,edgecolor="white"))
for offset,alpha in [(0,.45),(.18,.8)]:
    for i in range(3):
        for j in range(4): axes[3].add_patch(Rectangle((.1+j*.26+offset,.17+(2-i)*.26+offset),.24,.24,facecolor=purple,alpha=alpha,edgecolor="white"))
for ax in axes: ax.set_xlim(0,1.55); ax.set_ylim(0,1.45)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()



Figure 17.1



Rank counts axes, not the number of stored values and not the dimension of every vector contained in the array. A matrix with shape \(10{,}000\times768\) has rank two, while each of its 10,000 rows can be a 768-dimensional vector. Keeping these meanings separate prevents a common source of confusion.

Why this chapter matters
Many model inputs are discrete. A word type, product identifier, diagnosis code, or graph node is a category, not a measured quantity. Assigning integer identifiers makes these items indexable but creates a false numerical order: identifier 20 is not twice identifier 10, and adjacent identifiers need not be related.
An embedding assigns each item a learned vector. The vector gives later layers a compact set of coordinates on which dot products, distances, and transformations can operate. Those coordinates are fitted from an objective and a dataset. Their geometry therefore records statistical regularities and modelling choices, not an authoritative map of meaning.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

explain why integer identifiers are unsuitable as numerical features;
distinguish one-hot vectors from dense embeddings;
describe an embedding layer as a trainable lookup table;
construct centre-context pairs from a sequence and explain how they train vectors;
derive the softmax and negative-sampling objectives used in predictive word embeddings;
calculate dot products, Euclidean distances, and cosine similarities;
explain how vector norms and global geometry affect nearest neighbours;
distinguish static item embeddings from contextual representations;
interpret analogies and projections without treating coordinates as intrinsic concepts;
identify frequency, coverage, bias, anisotropy, and evaluation problems;
account for embedding memory, sparse updates, sharding, and tied weights;
design an embedding audit for research or production use.



Running corpus
The chapter uses a small constructed corpus containing animal and vehicle terms. Its purpose is to make co-occurrence counts, context windows, and fitted vectors inspectable. Results from this corpus illustrate mechanics; they do not provide evidence about natural-language semantics.




Code
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from matplotlib.patches import Rectangle, FancyArrowPatch

plt.style.use("seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid")
blue, teal, orange, purple, grey = "#24527a", "#3a9188", "#d97745", "#7a5195", "#5f6b76"

items=["cat","kitten","dog","puppy","car","truck"]
example_matrix=np.array([[.8,.2,-.1],[.7,.3,-.2],[.6,-.4,.1],[.55,-.5,.15],[-.5,.2,.8],[-.6,.1,.75]])
selected=3
fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(10,4.6)); ax.axis("off")
for i,name in enumerate(items):
    y=5.3-i*.78
    ax.text(.15,y,f"id {i}: {name}",va="center")
    for j,value in enumerate(example_matrix[i]):
        colour=orange if i==selected else blue
        ax.add_patch(Rectangle((2.0+j*.85,y-.27),.75,.54,facecolor=colour,alpha=.9,edgecolor="white"))
        ax.text(2.375+j*.85,y,f"{value:.2f}",ha="center",va="center",color="white")
ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch((4.75,5.3-selected*.78),(6.0,3.0),arrowstyle="-|&gt;",mutation_scale=16,color=grey))
for j,value in enumerate(example_matrix[selected]):
    ax.add_patch(Rectangle((6.1+j*.9,2.7),.8,.6,facecolor=orange,edgecolor="white"))
    ax.text(6.5+j*.9,3.0,f"{value:.2f}",ha="center",va="center")
ax.text(3.0,6.0,"trainable matrix E",ha="center",fontsize=12)
ax.text(7.4,3.55,"selected vector E[3]",ha="center",fontsize=11)
ax.set_xlim(-.1,9.1); ax.set_ylim(.5,6.35); plt.show()



Figure 18.1



The highlighted row is shared wherever the identifier 3 occurs. During training, gradients can change that row. The ordering of rows is an implementation choice; permuting the vocabulary and applying the same permutation to the matrix leaves the represented relationships unchanged.

Why this chapter matters
Chapter 18 showed the limitation of representing an entire source sequence with one final recurrent state. Different output decisions often need different parts of the source. Attention replaces that fixed access pattern with a query-dependent retrieval operation: score each available source representation, normalise the scores, and return a weighted combination of source values.
Attention is used well beyond translation. The same operation can retrieve from text, image regions, memory slots, database records, or previous model states. This chapter introduces the operation in an encoder-decoder setting because source positions, a decoder query, and a resulting context vector can be inspected separately. Chapter 20 applies the same ingredients when the sequence queries itself.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

distinguish queries, keys, values, scores, weights, and context vectors;
describe attention as query-dependent weighted retrieval;
calculate dot-product, bilinear, and additive alignment scores;
apply softmax to scores and explain how its normalisation affects gradients and competition;
construct a masked attention distribution for variable-length or causal data;
calculate a weighted value sum and identify its shape;
explain how changing the query changes which source information is retrieved;
distinguish global, local, causal, and cross-attention access patterns;
explain why attention weights are not automatically causal explanations;
identify numerical, memory, and latency costs in an attention implementation;
specify the masks, score functions, normalisation rules, and provenance needed to audit an attention mechanism.



Running example
The running example contains five encoder states, each with three value coordinates. A decoder state produces a query. Attention scores each encoder state against that query, converts the scores into weights, and returns a context vector. The values are constructed for explanation; they are not learned semantic vectors.




Code
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from matplotlib.patches import Rectangle, FancyArrowPatch

plt.style.use("seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid")
blue, teal, orange, purple, grey = "#24527a", "#3a9188", "#d97745", "#5f6b76"

fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(12,4.7)); ax.axis("off")
for i in range(5):
    x=.3+i*1.15
    ax.add_patch(Rectangle((x,3.0),.9,.58,facecolor=blue,edgecolor="white"))
    ax.text(x+.45,3.29,f"source {i+1}\nkey, value",ha="center",va="center",fontsize=8,color="white")
    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch((x+.45,2.95),(x+.45,2.32),arrowstyle="-|&gt;",mutation_scale=11,color=grey))
    ax.add_patch(Rectangle((x,1.75),.9,.43,facecolor=orange,edgecolor="white"))
    ax.text(x+.45,1.965,f"score {i+1}",ha="center",va="center",fontsize=8)
    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch((x+.45,1.7),(x+.45,1.15),arrowstyle="-|&gt;",mutation_scale=11,color=grey))
    ax.text(x+.45,.92,f"weight {i+1}",ha="center",fontsize=8,color=teal)
ax.add_patch(Rectangle((7.0,2.45),1.4,.62,facecolor=purple,edgecolor="white")); ax.text(7.7,2.76,"decoder query",ha="center",va="center",color="white")
for i in range(5): ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch((7.0,2.65),(5.25-i*1.15,2.2),arrowstyle="-|&gt;",mutation_scale=10,color=purple,alpha=.6))
ax.add_patch(Rectangle((7.1,.5),1.35,.58,facecolor=teal,edgecolor="white")); ax.text(7.775,.79,"context vector",ha="center",va="center",color="white")
for i in range(5): ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch((.75+i*1.15,.87),(7.05,.79),arrowstyle="-|&gt;",mutation_scale=9,color=teal,alpha=.38))
ax.text(3.0,.25,"softmax normalises scores; weighted values are summed",ha="center",color=grey)
ax.set_xlim(0,9); ax.set_ylim(0,4.1); plt.show()



Figure 20.1



The weights depend on the query. A later decoder state can therefore retrieve a different combination of the same source values. The source states remain available as separate objects instead of being exposed only through one fixed encoder vector.

Why this chapter matters
Chapter 19 defined attention between a decoder query and a source sequence. Self-attention uses the same operation within one sequence: every position produces a query, key, and value, then retrieves a context from eligible positions in that sequence. It replaces the recurrent requirement to pass information through every intervening state with direct query-key comparisons.
One self-attention head learns one projected retrieval pattern. Multi-head attention runs several such patterns in parallel and recombines their outputs. This chapter explains those operations. It does not supply positional information or the residual, normalisation, and feed-forward machinery of a complete Transformer block; Chapters 21 and 22 do that.


Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

distinguish cross-attention from self-attention;
write the shapes of projected queries, keys, values, scores, weights, and outputs;
derive scaled dot-product self-attention;
explain why causal masking enforces an autoregressive information boundary;
explain what self-attention does and does not know about order without positional information;
describe multi-head attention as parallel learned projection-and-retrieval paths;
implement and test a masked multi-head attention module;
account for its quadratic score matrix and key-value cache implications;
avoid treating heads or attention maps as self-explanatory explanations.



Running sequence
The running tensor has one batch item, five positions, and four input features. It is constructed to make position-to-position weights visible. The values are illustrative rather than learned language representations.




